{"subjectId":"python","id":"testing","title":"Testing: unittest & pytest","group":"Testing & Tooling","order":61,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Automated tests prove your code works today and keep it working tomorrow. Python ships <code>unittest</code> and <code>doctest</code>; most projects use <code>pytest</code> for its plain <code>assert</code>s and fixtures.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-target-arrow\"></i>Why and what to test</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cube\"></i>Unit tests</h3><p>One function or class in isolation. Fast (milliseconds) — the bulk of your suite.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug-connected\"></i>Integration tests</h3><p>Several parts together: your code + a real database, file system or HTTP layer.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-world\"></i>End-to-end tests</h3><p>The whole system as a user sees it. Slow and brittle — keep few of them.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug-off\"></i>Regression tests</h3><p>Every fixed bug gets a test that failed before the fix, so it can't come back.</p></div></div>\n<p>A good test follows <strong>Arrange → Act → Assert</strong>: set up inputs, call the code once, check the result.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i>assert — the building block</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def slugify(title: str) -&gt; str:\n    return \"-\".join(title.lower().split())\n\n# Arrange, Act, Assert — a test is just code that raises if something is wrong\nassert slugify(\"Hello World\") == \"hello-world\"\nassert slugify(\"  Python   Notes \") == \"python-notes\"\ntry:\n    assert slugify(\"A B\") == \"a_b\", \"separator should be a hyphen\"\nexcept AssertionError as e:\n    print(\"failed:\", e)             # failed: separator should be a hyphen</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>assert</code> statements are removed when Python runs with <code>-O</code>. Use them in tests and for internal sanity checks — never to validate user input in production code.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>unittest — the built-in framework</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import io, unittest\n\nclass Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._items = []\n    def push(self, x):\n        self._items.append(x)\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self._items:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from empty stack\")\n        return self._items.pop()\n\nclass StackTest(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):                         # runs before EACH test method\n        self.s = Stack()\n\n    def test_push_then_pop(self):\n        self.s.push(1)\n        self.s.push(2)\n        self.assertEqual(self.s.pop(), 2)\n\n    def test_pop_empty_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(IndexError):\n            self.s.pop()\n\n    def test_many_values(self):\n        for value in [0, \"x\", None]:\n            with self.subTest(value=value):  # each case reported separately\n                self.s.push(value)\n                self.assertIs(self.s.pop(), value)\n\nsuite = unittest.defaultTestLoader.loadTestsFromTestCase(StackTest)\nresult = unittest.TextTestRunner(stream=io.StringIO(), verbosity=0).run(suite)\nprint(result.testsRun, len(result.failures), len(result.errors))   # 3 0 0\nprint(result.wasSuccessful())                                        # True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Assertion</th><th>Checks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>assertEqual(a, b)</code> / <code>assertNotEqual</code></td><td><code>a == b</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>assertTrue(x)</code> / <code>assertFalse(x)</code></td><td>Truthiness</td></tr><tr><td><code>assertIs(a, b)</code> / <code>assertIsNone(x)</code></td><td>Identity</td></tr><tr><td><code>assertIn(a, b)</code></td><td>Membership</td></tr><tr><td><code>assertRaises(E)</code></td><td>The block raises <code>E</code> (use as a context manager)</td></tr><tr><td><code>assertAlmostEqual(a, b, places=7)</code></td><td>Floats close enough</td></tr><tr><td><code>assertCountEqual(a, b)</code></td><td>Same items, any order</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>setUp</code>/<code>tearDown</code> run around every test; <code>setUpClass</code>/<code>tearDownClass</code> once per class. Test methods must start with <code>test</code>. Run everything with <code>python -m unittest discover</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flask\"></i>pytest — less boilerplate</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># test_cart.py\nimport pytest\nfrom shop.cart import Cart\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef cart():                                   # fresh object for every test that asks for it\n    c = Cart()\n    c.add(\"pen\", price=10, qty=2)\n    return c\n\ndef test_total(cart):\n    assert cart.total() == 20                 # plain assert — pytest shows both sides on failure\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"qty, expected\", [(1, 10), (3, 30), (0, 0)])\ndef test_line_total(qty, expected):\n    c = Cart()\n    c.add(\"pen\", price=10, qty=qty)\n    assert c.total() == expected\n\ndef test_negative_qty_rejected():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"qty\"):\n        Cart().add(\"pen\", price=10, qty=-1)\n\ndef test_export(cart, tmp_path):              # built-in fixture: a fresh temp directory\n    path = tmp_path / \"cart.json\"\n    cart.save(path)\n    assert path.exists()</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ pip install pytest\n$ pytest                       # finds test_*.py files and test_* functions\n$ pytest -q -k \"total\"         # quiet, only tests whose name matches\n$ pytest -x --lf               # stop at first failure; rerun last failures\n$ pytest --cov=shop            # coverage report (pytest-cov plugin)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>pytest output</figcaption><pre><code>============================= test session starts ==============================\ncollected 6 items\n\ntest_cart.py ......                                                      [100%]\n\n============================== 6 passed in 0.02s ===============================</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>unittest</code></th><th><code>pytest</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Install</td><td>Built in</td><td><code>pip install pytest</code></td></tr><tr><td>Style</td><td>Classes + <code>self.assertX</code> methods</td><td>Plain functions + <code>assert</code></td></tr><tr><td>Setup</td><td><code>setUp</code> / <code>tearDown</code></td><td>Fixtures, injected by name, with scopes</td></tr><tr><td>Parametrise</td><td><code>subTest</code></td><td><code>@pytest.mark.parametrize</code></td></tr><tr><td>Runs the other's tests?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes — pytest runs unittest suites too</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-file-text\"></i>doctest — examples that are tests</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import doctest, io\n\ndef median(xs):\n    \"\"\"Return the median of a non-empty list.\n\n    &gt;&gt;&gt; median([3, 1, 2])\n    2\n    &gt;&gt;&gt; median([4, 1, 3, 2])\n    2.5\n    \"\"\"\n    s = sorted(xs)\n    mid = len(s) // 2\n    return s[mid] if len(s) % 2 else (s[mid - 1] + s[mid]) / 2\n\nrunner = doctest.DocTestRunner()\nfor test in doctest.DocTestFinder().find(median, globs={\"median\": median}):\n    runner.run(test, out=io.StringIO().write)\nprint(runner.tries, runner.failures)       # 2 0</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Terminal</figcaption><pre><code>$ python -m doctest -v stats.py     # run every docstring example in a module\n$ pytest --doctest-modules          # or let pytest collect them</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>Coverage &amp; the TDD loop</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Red</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Write a small failing test for the next bit of behaviour.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Green</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Write the simplest code that makes it pass.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Refactor</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Clean up with the tests as a safety net; rerun them.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Coverage (<code>coverage run -m pytest &amp;&amp; coverage report</code>) shows which lines never ran. High coverage doesn't prove correctness, but <strong>uncovered code is definitely untested</strong>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Keep tests fast, independent and deterministic; one behaviour per test; descriptive names like <code>test_pop_empty_raises</code>.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Tests that depend on each other's order, the current time, the network or random data without a fixed seed.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a fixture in pytest?</summary><div><p>A function decorated with <code>@pytest.fixture</code> that prepares something a test needs (an object, a temp directory, a DB connection). Tests receive it by naming it as a parameter. Fixtures can <code>yield</code> to run teardown and have scopes (<code>function</code>, <code>module</code>, <code>session</code>).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>unittest or pytest?</summary><div><p>pytest for most projects: less boilerplate, better failure output, fixtures and a large plugin ecosystem — and it runs existing unittest tests. unittest when you can't add dependencies.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you test code that raises an exception?</summary><div><p><code>with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"...\"):</code> or <code>with self.assertRaises(ValueError):</code> around the call. The test fails if the exception is <em>not</em> raised.</p></div></details></section>"}